body:
Hello, I'm vasilich-tregub, let me feel invited,

Contrary to annotation "Please leave your sense of logic at the door. All are welcome to participate" (https://github.com/whatwg), "sense" is mentioned in posts of this issue. You see it in phrases 'it would make most sense to specify such details' and 'based on common-sense consensus'. Outside the long-standing discussion about 'execCommand': I wonder, does 'makes most sense to specify the behavior for non-text node deletion' mean that the only question is to what group assign the task 'to specify such details'? Also, if there is a 'common-sense consensus', may it lessen the necessity 'to specify such details'?

From the point of view of audience, which is defined in https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#audience as 'authors of documents and scripts that use the features defined in this specification, ...', there are a lot of seemingly 'undefined behaviors' in this specification. For example, let we author a document like

```
<!DOCTYPE html>

<html lang="en" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>contenteditable true</title>
    <style>
        body {font-size: 200%;}
        div.outer {
            min-width: 3em;
            min-height: 4em;
            background-color: lavender;
            align-content: center;
        }

        div.inner {
            min-width: 3em;
            min-height: 2em;
            background-color: azure;
            align-content: center;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <p style="color: purple;">colored symbols</p>
    <p>and an <span style="font-style: italic;">emphasized</span> word</p>
    <div class="outer" contenteditable="true"><div class="inner">___</div></div>
    <script>
        const outerdiv = document.querySelector("div.outer")
        const innerdiv = document.querySelector("div.inner")

        outerdiv.addEventListener("keyup", (e) => {
            console.log(outerdiv.outerHTML);
            console.log(innerdiv.innerText.split(""));
        });
    </script>
</body>

</html>

```
open it in Microsoft Edge v150 and test by copying `colored symbols` and `and an emphasized word` strings from top paras to `<div class="inner">___</div>`. Clicking the inner div and pressing an arrow key result in console.log printout:

```
<div class="outer" contenteditable="true">
<div class="inner">_<span style="color: rgb(128, 0, 128); background-color: lavender;"> colored symbols</span>_
<span style="background-color: lavender;"> and an</span><span style="background-color: lavender;"> </span>
<span style="font-style: italic;">emphasized</span><span style="background-color: lavender;"> </span>
<span style="background-color: lavender;">word</span>_</div></div>
► ['_', ' ', 'c', 'o', 'l', 'o', 'r', 'e', 'd', ' ', 's', 'y', 'm', 'b', 'o', 'l', 's', '_', ' ', 'a', 'n', 'd', ' ', 
   'a', 'n', ' ', 'e', 'm', 'p', 'h', 'a', 's', 'i', 'z', 'e', 'd', ' ', 'w', 'o', 'r', 'd', '_']
```

Why, on having been copied, textnodes `colored symbols`, `and an `, and ` word` fetch with them the background-color value of the parent DIV (which is outside the source paras) to inline styles of the target DIV children? Why `emphasized` do not follow suit? Why drag-n-dropping the `and an emphasized word` selection adds a leading space to the copied buffer, while copy-pasting it does not do the same? Why a stroke color, which is named 'purple' in the style of the string `colored symbols`, on inserting into the target nodes maps to RGB color `color: rgb(128, 0, 128);`, while background-color's values in styles remain expressed as color names?

These questions of mine might sound somewhat rhetoric. I'm sure: logically impeccable answers, with due diligence, can be researched and traced in specs. Only, for the intended audience the questions of this sort are not exercise in rhetorics, but still often hard to answer. And, quite often, the behavior is not what is intuitively expected by many authors. Fortunately, MDN is to the rescue, and, of course, experimentation is a swiss army knife of authors of documents and scripts.

To finish -- I fear, my somewhat lengthy and maybe out-of-topic comment: I believe, the originator posted a valuable observation about the state of spec completeness, but there is little what the community can do to emend whatever specs concerning, specifically, interoperability of non-text node deletion behavior. If relying on interoperability fail, the originator would know through issues filed by users of his rich text editor product and be able to improve his design using current or future APIs.
